// ADR 0071 M1.4b slice 2b-ii: `lock(m) -> ?Guard<T>` reaches TYPE + LOWERING in
// both compilers. `lock` acquires the mutex with the always-on LockTimeout tier
// and returns `?Guard` — a guard on success, None on timeout (the `recv` `?T`
// shape, D4 fallible). This brings `Type::Guard` + the `NullableInner::Guard`
// (`?Guard` lowers to `{ i1, ptr }`, like `?ptr`) + `is_some` over a `?Guard` into
// every self-host differential stage.
//
// DELIBERATE LEAK (slice-3a soundness): the mutex is created as an UNBOUND rvalue
// temporary passed straight to `lock`, so it is never scope-exit-dropped (no
// `sentinel_mutex_release`). That is required until slice 3b wires the guard's
// unlock-on-drop: `lock` holds the lock (the runtime guard is forgotten across the
// C-ABI boundary), and releasing a still-locked cell would trip the runtime's
// free-while-locked debug-assert. Binding the mutex + dropping it (the refcount
// path) is covered separately by `c71_mutex_rc.sentinel`; the `*g` deref + the
// guard's unlock-on-drop that make a bound-and-locked mutex sound are slice 3b.
//
// The (uncontended) lock succeeds → `is_some` is true → 42. Exit 42.

fn main() -> i64 {
    if is_some(lock(mutex_new(42))) {
        42
    } else {
        0
    }
}
